# slopgate.patterns: the words slopgate.sh rejects, kept where they can be read.
#
# History
#   2026-08-07  A. Sigdel  Created, from the constants at the top of slopgate.sh.
#   2026-08-10  A. Sigdel  The dashes, and twenty-one words the strip made eligible.
#
# Sourced by slopgate.sh, and the only file that guard excludes from the diff it
# reads. It has to exclude something: a list of forbidden words contains every
# word on it, so the file defining the list always matches itself.
#
# Splitting the list out of the script is what keeps that exclusion honest. It
# used to cover all of slopgate.sh, which is two hundred lines of prose about the
# register: the file most likely to drift into it, and the one file the guard
# could never catch drifting. Only these four lines have to be exempt; the rest of
# that script is now read like anything else.
#
# On adding a word: it is eligible only while this repository's own prose does not
# already use it. That rule is the whole defence of the list, and applying it cost
# five candidates. `additionally`, `significant`, `notably`, `unlock` and
# `landscape` are all in files written here, so none of them can be banned. Check
# before adding.
#
# The rule is also why the dashes are here now and could not have been before.
# They were on 879 lines across 229 files, which is a guard that reports the whole
# repository on its first run and is therefore a guard nobody keeps. #582 took
# them out over seven pull requests; this is what stops the next one arriving.

# Marketing register: words that describe a thing rather than say it.
readonly REGISTER='leverages?|leveraging|utili[sz]e[sd]?|delve[sd]?|seamless(ly)?|robust|comprehensive|powerful|plethora|myriad|elevates?|boasts|testament|cutting-edge|state-of-the-art|best-in-class|meticulous(ly)?|furthermore|moreover|crucial|pivotal|underscores|holistic|synerg|streamline[sd]?|empower(s|ed|ing)?|bespoke|essentially|fundamentally|realm|tapestry|showcase[sd]?|facilitate[sd]?|revolutioni|it is important to|a wide range of|the world of|in order to'

# Chat-context shorthand: phrases addressed to a conversation rather than to a
# reader who was not in it.
readonly SHORTHAND="it.?s worth noting|as mentioned (above|earlier)|as (we|you) (discussed|saw)|in (summary|conclusion)|deep dive|dive into|at the end of the day|needless to say|ever-evolving|in today.s|game.chang|under the hood|first-class citizen"

# Attribution, matched by shape rather than by name. The standard forbids naming a
# tool, so a guard that grepped for product names would be the first file here to
# break the rule it enforces.
readonly ATTRIBUTION='co-authored-by:|generated (with|by) \['

# Markers. A marker naming an issue is a plan; one naming nothing is a note to
# somebody who has already left.
readonly MARKER='\b(TODO|FIXME|HACK|XXX)\b'

# The em-dash and the en-dash, as UTF-8 bytes for the reason EMOJI is: the match
# must not depend on the locale grep was handed. E2 80 94 and E2 80 93.
#
# Bytes rather than the marks themselves buys a second thing, and it is the one
# worth stating. Written literally, this file would be the single place in the
# repository still holding them, and `git grep` for either would answer with the
# guard that bans them forever after. Written as bytes, the answer stays zero and
# means what it says.
readonly DASHES=$'\xe2\x80\x94|\xe2\x80\x93'
